Problem 3(a):

Prove Hintikka's Lemma.

1: Suppose some set S is a Hintikka set. We are to show that this set is satisfiable. To do this, it suffices to show that, using mathematical induction, all formulas of all possible degrees in S are true under one interpretation. For the formulae of degree 0, which are simply propositional variables, we give the value of truth to any T X. Then, for any F X, we give the value of falsehood. This ensures that, whichever type of variable is in S, X will be true (It will be true because if X is of type T, we are assigning it the value truth, which is true, and thus X is true. If F X is true, then we will assign X the value false, and then each F X will be true, as X is false). Thus, under this interpreation I, all formulas of degree 0 are true, proving the base case. 
2: Now assume that all the formulas of size n are true under I. Any formulas of degree n+1 are either of type alpha or beta. Because S is a hintikka set, all a1 and a2, or b1 and b2 are members of S if an alpha or beta formula is a member of S. So, assume that a formula of degree n+1 is of type alpha. Then a1 and a2 are members of S. Because a1 and a2 are one degree less than a by definition, a1 and a2 are true under I, and thus a itself is true under I.
3: Now Assume that a formula of degree n+1 is of type beta. Then either b1 and b2 are members of S, and one of them is true under I (because they are also one degree less than n+1). Because either b1 and b2 is true under I, b is of course also true under I, completing the proof.

Altenrnatively, the proof can be done by simply starting at step 2, but using complete mathematical induction instead of mathematical induction. This ensures that proving the base case of formulas of degree 0 is not necessary, and thus step 1 can be left out. (Shown below)

1: Suppose some set S is a Hintikka set. We are to show this set is satisfiable by complete mathematical induction. Assume that all formulas of degree n or less are true under some interpretation I. We are to show that all formulas of degree n+1 are also true under I. All formulae of degree n+1 are either of type A or type B (the formulae of degrees n+1 are at least of degree 1, and thus have at least one connective, making them necessarily of either type A or B.). Assume X is a formula of degree n+1 and is of type A. Because S is a Hintikka set, both a1 and a2 are also members of S. Because a1 and a2 are both of degrees less than A, they are true under I, making X also true under I. Now Assume X is of type B. Again, because S is a Hintikka set, either b1 and b2 are members of S, and because they are of degrees less than n+1, one of them is true under I, thus making X also true under I, completing the proof.

Problem 3(b):

Prove that if a signed formula X is unsatisfiable, then every completed tableau for X must be closed.

If a formula X is unsatisfiable, then X is not true under any interpretation I. Now consider some completed tableua for X. Because any open branch of a completed tableua is a Hintikka set, it follows that any open branch of a completed tableau must be satisfiable. But, because the unsatisfiable formula X is at the top of the tableau, and thus is included in every possible branch, no possible branch of this tableau could be satisfiable, and thus every completed tableau for X must be closed.

Problem 3(c):

Prove that if an unsigned formula X is a tautology, then any completed tableau for F X must be closed. 

If X is a tautology, it then immediately follows that F X must be unsatisfiable. Because F X, a singed formula, is unsatisfiable, every completed tableau for F X must be closed by 3(b).

Problem 4:

Prove that every finite subset of S is satisfiable iff for every n, the set {X1,...,Xn} is satisfiable.

1: First, we assume that every finite subset of S is satisfiable. We must show that for every n, the set {X1,...Xn} is satisfiable. Because X1,...Xn clearly ends at some point, namely Xn, it is a finite subset of S. Because all finite subsets of S were assumed to be true, {X1,...Xn} is also clearly true, completing the first step of the proof.
2: Conversley, assume that for every n, the set {X1,...,Xn} is satisfiable. Now, consider any subset S1 of S, in which n is the greatest number such that Xn is an element of S1. Thus S1 is a subset of the set {X1,...,Xn}. Because {X1,...,Xn} was assumed to be satsifiable, all of its subsets are also satisfiable, meaning S1 is satisfiable, thus completing the proof.



Problem 5(a):

Prove that if a set S is both satisfiable and full, then S is a truth set.

Because S is satisfiable, every element of S is true under some interpretion I. Because S is full, for every formula X, either X or its conjugate ~X is in S, but not both. We must now show that every formula which is true under I is a member of S. Assume that some formula X is true under I. Then, because all elements of S are true under I, X must be a member of S (if it was not, ~X would be a member of S by definition of S being a full set, making S contain an element not true under I, which is a contradiction). Because all X that are true under I must be member of S, S is clearly a truth set for I.


