Problem 6:

Prove that for any set S of signed formulas and any signed formula X, if some finite subset of S U {X} is unsatisfiable, and if some finite subset of S U {X~} is unsatisfiable, then some finite subset of S is unsatisfiable. The unsatisfiable finite subset of S U {X} is either some set F1 U {X} where F1 is a finite subset of S, or F1 itself, where F1 U {X} is obviously also unsatsifiable (because X is a member of F1). Similarly, the unsatisfiable finite subset of S U {X~} is either some set F2 U {X} where F2 is a finite subset of S, or F2 itself, where F2 U {X} is also unsatisfiable. Now consider the finite subset of S F3, which is the union of F1 and F2. Because F1 U {X} is unsatisfiable, F3 U {X} is also unsatisfiable. Similarly, because F2 U {X~} is unsatisfiable, F3 U {X~} is also unsatisfiable. Then, consider some interpretation I in which the set F3 is true, in other words, assume the set F3 is satisfiable. Either X or X~ would be true under I. But, because there is no interpretation in which the union of F3 and X or X~ is satisfiable, it is obvious that F3 itself cannot be satisfiable under I (if it was, then either F3 U {X} or F3 U {X~} would be satisfiable under I, because either X or X~ is satisfiable under I), thus yeilding a contradiction. Thus, because F3 cannot be true under any interpretation I, it follows that F3 is unsatisfiable, and is the finite subset of S which is unsatisfiable.

Alternatively, we can prove this statement, which is equivalent to the above statement: If all finite subsets of S are satisfiable, then either all finite subsets of S U {X} are satisfiable, or all finite subsets of S U {X~} are satisfiable. 

Because all finite subsets of S are satisfiable, it follows that under some interpretation I, all finite subsets of S are true. Under the interpretation I, either X or its conjugate X~ must be true by definition. Because of this, either the set S U {X} or S U {X~} contains the form of the formula X (either X or X~) which is satisfiable under I; now we must consider all the finite subsets of that set. A finite subset of this set is either a finite subset of S where X is not a member of it; a finite subset of S in disjunction with X or X~; a finite subset of S that includes X or X~ (if X or X~ is a member of S), or X or X~ itself. The first case it satisfiable by the assumption that all finite subsets of S are satisfiable. The second case is satisfiable because the form of X that is satisfiable under I was assumed to be in this set; the third case is satisfiable because all finite subsets of S were assumed to be satisfiable; the fourth case is satisfiable because the form of X in this set was assumed to be true under I, and thus satisfiable. Thus either all finite subsets of the set S U {~X} or S U {X} are satisfiable, completing the proof.


