Problem 8:

Prove that if a formula X is a logical consequence of a set S of formulas, then X is also a logical consequence of some finite subet of S.

By the compactness theorem for propositional logic, some set S having property P (Being true whenever the set S is true), and some formula X having property P as well, means that the set containing both S and X has property P. Thus, by the compactness theorem for propositional logic, all finite subsets of S U {X} have property P, meaning all finite subset of S have property P. This obviously means that some finite subset F of S, and the formula X, both have property P (because S U {X} has the property P). Thus, the set {F,X} has the property P, and therefore X is a logical consequence of F, a finite subset of S. 


Alternatively, because X is a logical consequence of S, S U {X~} is clearly unsatisfiable. Then, by the compacntess theorem for propositional logic, some finite subset S1 of S U {X~} is unsatisfiable. Thus, S1 U {X~} is unsatisfiable. Thus it is obvious that X must be true whenever S1 is true, and therefore X is a logical consequence of the finite subset S1 of S.

