Exercise 4(b):

Give a direct proof that if X is a tautology, then every completed dual tableau for T X is closed. 

(a): First, we must prove that if a dual tableau T is open, then there exists at least one branch which is a dual Hintikka set. Condition H0(0) is obvious, as by definition of T being open, T must have at least one branch being open. This open branch, by definition of being open, cannot have a formula X and its conjugate X~ on the same branch, thus proving H0(0) holds. As for conditions H1(0) and H2(0), we will prove these by mathematical induction. For the base case where X itself is of type a or b, because the dual tableua method consists of branching off any formula of type a into two branches containing a1 and a2, and adding b1 and b2 as direct consequences for any b, any branch containg the base formula X where X is of type a will have either a1 or a2 as well, and will have both b1 and b2 if X is of type b; thus H1(0) holds for the 0th level of the tree, and H2(0) holds for the 0th level of the tree. proving the base case. Assume that H1(0) and H2(0) hold for some nth level of the tree. Now, consider a formula of degree n+1. If it is of type a, again, the dual tableau method consists of branching a's into two branches of a1 or a2, meaning either of the two branches continuing with this formula of degree n+1 would contain either a1 or a2. As for a formula of type b, because the dual tableau method consists of adding both b1 and b2 to the branch where b is on, both b1 and b2 will be on any branch with b. Thus H1(0) and H2(0) hold for all n, meaning that if a dual tableau T is open, every branch of T is a dual Hintikka set.

(b): Now we consider some tautology X, and we must show that every tableau starting with X is closed. Well, because X is true under all interpretations, and the fact that X is a member of all branches on any tableau that starts with X (because X is at the top of the tableau), it is obvious that there is no branch in any tableau starting with X that can have all it's members being false under some interpretation, meaning no branch of a tableau T starting with X is a dual Hintikka set. Because no tableau starting with X has any branches as a dual Hintikka set, by (a) T cannot be open, and thus T must be closed. 

